00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
having spent the better part of a month  with the xbox series x  preview unit and now about a week with  an official retail build  i've been able to experience the breadth  of what microsoft's next-gen system  offers  i've covered its capabilities in great  detail with two full previews but now  it's time for a full review with the  retail unit  the xbox series x is an impressive piece  of hardware one that flexes its power in  subtle yet meaningful ways but i  wouldn't say it's essential to  transition to the series x at launch  if you're on a 1x but the benefits of  the next-gen hardware  are tangible and you won't want to go  back once you get hands-on  its rdna 2 gpu and zentu cpu from amd  the series x makes a significant  technical leap for current gen games  played through backwards compatibility  this means  unlocking untapped potential that the  one x couldn't leverage  entirely and in some cases series x does  this without any need for optimizations  some games will get inherent performance  boost by virtue of the more powerful  specs  so if a game runs on an unlocked frame  rate or a 60fps cap that it couldn't  always maintain on existing hardware you  will see the game hit those higher frame  rates more consistently and if a game  runs a dynamic resolution it'll be able  to maintain the highest resolutions it's  allowed to hit  previous examples i've used are final  fantasy xv and monster hunter world both  of which have 1x enhancements but not  series x enhancements in final fantasy  15 its performance mode runs a  consistent 60fps and monster hunter  world can do the same  even on the prioritized graphics mode  where the 1x would struggle to maintain  60fps the series x lets these games  reach that potential  sekiro shadow's die twice is probably  one of the best examples of series x  hardware  simply boosting a game's performance to  hit 60fps unlike the xbox one x  this is a case where it genuinely and  meaningfully improves the experience  since sekiro's an intense high-stakes  action game on the flip side an example  of hard-coded limitations in games  final fantasy xv's higher fidelity  options still run at its enforced cap of  30 fps and the same applies to games  like the outer worlds  so don't go in expecting every single  game to be running 4k resolution or  super high frame rates  some games are just made with built-in  resolution or frame rate caps  in those cases it's up to developers if  they want to go back and implement new  optimizations to utilize the new power  afforded by the series  x  [Music]  as for series x optimized games we've  had access to gears 5 gears tactics  forza horizon 4  sea of thieves and yakuza like a dragon  with gears 5  you can boost the frame rate to 120 fps  in multiplayer to take advantage of the  console's 4k 120hz capabilities  if you have the proper display the  enhancements i appreciate more however  come from the increases from 30fps to  60fps while running crispy 4k  such as the case in gears tactics forza  horizon 4 and sea of thieves  they simply run full 4k 60fps as opposed  to the xbox one x graphics options where  you'd have to choose between visuals or  performance  yakuza like dragon is a bit trickier and  you get two options available  high resolution for native 4k 30fps and  normal  for 1440p 60fps while it's likely to be  a case-by-case basis this could be  indicative of where more graphically  demanding games may go for the upcoming  generation  from a technical standpoint although the  changes may not blow you away  these games have been slick experiences  with some noticeable visual improvements  however it's a fairly limited showcase  of the series x's full power we should  expect more substantial enhancements as  more of these optimizations roll out  [Music]  super fast loading is what i think  people will be most impressed by as it  provides a more practical benefit that  makes gaming experiences nearly seamless  the internal nvme ssd does the heavy  lifting and it does so without needing  specific optimizations  the series x can load from the main menu  into gameplay for  red dead redemption 2 for example in  around 30 seconds  games like control final fantasy xv and  the outer worlds load in about 10 to 12  seconds  and gears 5 and gears tactics load into  action in about 5 seconds and 15 seconds  respectively yakuza like dragon can load  into its detailed world  in just 6 seconds these are all vast  improvements over the xbox one x's  internal storage drive that usually took  about four to five times  longer these numbers can start to lose  meaning when rattled off like this but  it can't be understated how much of a  difference the speed makes  fast load times like these have been a  major perk for pc gamers with nvme ssds  and now it's the standard going into the  new console generation  and it's a luxury you can enjoy here  the internal ssd sure is fast but 802  gigabytes of free space on this one  terabyte drive can fill up quickly with  the size of games today if you're  looking at options for expanding storage  the one terabyte seagate expansion card  is the premium  option although it's pricey at 220 bucks  at launch it performs  exactly like the internal drive you can  also use external usb 3 drives to play  backwards compatible games that haven't  been optimized for series x  if they are optimized or series x  specific games you'll have to transfer  them from external storage onto the  internal drive  or expansion card thankfully transfer  speeds are relatively fast over usb3  though that also depends on whether or  not you're using an ssd or a traditional  hard drive  [Music]  those of you who've been using the xbox  one for some time will notice that  things haven't changed all that much  when it comes to the user interface  navigation is much snappier on the  series x but it maintains the overall  look and feel which i personally don't  mind since it works well enough  what really brings that snappy  navigation into its own is the quick  resume  feature it's one of the highlights of  using the xbox series x you can have  around five to six game states suspended  and jump between those games  without having to reboot them all you do  is hit the home button and switch to  another game and quick resume takes care  of the rest it's built into how the  system operates  quick resume retains those suspended  states after turning off the console and  even after unplugging the power cord  now not everyone will jump between that  many games but it's really convenient  for hopping into the ones that you're  frequently playing  it's had some hiccups during the review  process with rare occasions of freezing  while trying to jump back into a game  the hope is that these issues can be  resolved if and when they do come up  because quick resume has a real benefit  for the way i play games just be aware  that the system doesn't communicate when  quick resume reaches its upper limit  though  along with the series x package is a  revised controller it doesn't stray too  far from the xbox one design but  features nice tactile textures on the  grips and triggers a crunchy 8-way d-pad  and a dedicated share button that lets  you take screenshots or record short  clips on the fly  you can also choose which resolution it  records in but that also dictates how  long these clips can be  as for the console design itself yeah it  has that thick brick aesthetic but it's  a simple and clean look  what's more important is that the system  has been whisper quiet throughout my  entire experience  and really you don't need to worry about  heat as long as you give the top fan  enough space to vent  so overall the xbox series x is a  fantastic console that still has a ton  of potential to unlock  until console exclusive games come out  you won't necessarily be gated from  accessing any content  xbox is offering holding off until you  start seeing more games make the most of  the system's potential  is a fair and smart move and nothing  really screams out as a killer app for  the series x right now  but it's also worth considering that  buying a console is an investment for  your gaming future  and not just for what's available here  and now and at least what we've seen  thus far is an enticing showcase  of series x's capabilities it's an  extremely refined xbox and a great piece  of tech that sets a strong foundation  for microsoft to better serve its  console users  at least right now the future looks  bright for the xbox platform  with the series x  you

